text:
taylor: Heads-up — ran into a nasty TS/packaging interaction when our SDK is consumed from an Edge (ESM) bundle. Generics and overloads disappear and everything shows up as any. Repro attached: https://github.com/redwood/repro-edge-ts-weird

mei: ouch. smallest repro pls?

taylor: Minimal SDK surface below. Works fine when consumer uses Node/CJS. Breaks after bundling for Edge with esbuild and tsconfig moduleResolution=bundler.

```ts
// packages/sdk/src/api.ts
export function transform<T>(input: T): Promise<{ out: T }>;
export function transform(input: unknown) { return Promise.resolve({ out: input as any }); }
```

consumer:
```ts
import { transform } from '@redwood/sdk';

type Req = { id: string };
async function run(){
  const r = await transform<Req>({ id: '1' });
  // expected r.out -> Req, but in ESM edge bundle r.out is any (no completions)
}
```

kevin: do you see different .d.ts in the package for cjs vs esm?

taylor: yes. dist/cjs/index.d.ts preserves overloads. dist/esm/index.d.ts in the tarball has a single widened signature (input: any). The publish pipeline runs a short ESM wrapper that re-exports internals: `export * from './lib'`. Seems like the declaration emitter or dts bundler flattened overloads.

maria: common culprits: 1) bundler-generated d.ts overwrote curated types, 2) types field/exports map points at a file whose .d.ts was produced by a tool that lost overloads, 3) tsconfig differences (moduleResolution: bundler, preserveValueImports) change declaration resolution. Also check typesVersions.

mei: quick diagnostic: untar the package and compare the two index.d.ts files. If the esm one is missing overloads then packaging is the issue. Also run `npx tsc --traceResolution` in a tiny consumer to see which d.ts is picked.

kevin: short-term workaround for users: deep-import the cjs entry (sad but true): `import { transform } from '@redwood/sdk/dist/cjs'` or pin a version we know is good. We'll add a note in the README.

taylor: i'll add a vercel edge fixture to our monorepo that installs the published tarball and runs `npx tsc --noEmit` so we catch regressions.

maria: long-term fixes: a) change exports.import to point at the compiled esm file that already has correct d.ts (avoid the thin re-export wrapper), b) run api-extractor or dts-bundle-generator to produce a curated index.d.ts that preserves overloads, or c) maintain a hand-authored index.d.ts for the public surface. Also add CI that emits declarations for both cjs and esm and diffs them.

packager-bot: Created issue RW-ENG-4999: "ESM packaging for Edge consumers flattens SDK overloads & widens generics". Attached repro and d.ts diffs.

mei: small thing we discovered in repro: when tsconfig uses `module: "esnext"` + `moduleResolution: "bundler"`, certain re-export patterns cause TypeScript to resolve to the wrapper's declaration only. If the wrapper doesn't re-declare overloads, TS can only see the widened impl signature.

kevin: i'll pick this up. Plan: 1) CI check that emits declarations for both package entries and fails on structural diffs, 2) change packaging to preserve original .d.ts or produce curated d.ts via api-extractor, 3) publish a patch and a docs troubleshooting note.

taylor: thanks — i'll attach the tiny consumer test and the tarball diff to the issue. Also adding a short snippet for users to detect the problem: `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` in a fixture that imports our esm entry.

maria: good call. i'll add the README blurb and link the issue. :thumbsup:
